; A head sigil whose form ends the list *is* that list, which is what keeps
; `(& x)` the borrow it has always been (`D-149`). So a parameter list holding
; one borrowed type keeps its parentheses, and writing it short says something
; else — the borrow, whose `&` then reaches type position on its own.
(fn apply ((f (Fn (&String) i64))) -> i64
  (f "hello"))

(fn main () -> i32 0)
